{
  "result": "At dawn, the city belongs to people who are usually invisible. Bakers lift warm trays from ovens, nurses trade tired smiles at bus stops, and street sweepers draw patient circles around yesterday\u2019s confetti. Windows brighten one by one, not dramatically, but with the modest certainty of stars appearing in reverse.\n\nI like this hour because nothing has fully decided what it will become. The day is still loose clay. A missed train might lead to a conversation; a sudden shower might send strangers beneath the same narrow awning. Even familiar roads seem capable of small betrayals, turning toward gardens, demolition sites, or a mural that did not exist last week.\n\nWe often imagine that meaning arrives with trumpets: the acceptance letter, the wedding toast, the long awaited call. Yet most of life gathers quietly. It accumulates in cups washed after dinner, names remembered, doors held open, and messages sent to ask whether someone got home safely. These gestures are too ordinary for monuments, but they are the hidden architecture of trust.\n\nMaybe that is why mornings feel hopeful. They do not promise perfection. They offer repetition with variation, another chance to practice attention. The same sun returns, but it touches different rooftops. The same coffee steams, but today\u2019s thoughts rise from it.\n\nIf I could choose one useful ambition, it would be to notice more: the sparrow balancing on a wire, the joke shared by commuters, the brief gold edge of a cloud, and the world beginning without applause again.",
